and you tonight after eight years a  nevada woman says her voice has finally  been heard helen armstrong says she lost  her job after reporting what she calls  unsafe practices at a local medical  office but this week the ninth circuit  appeals court ruled in her favor and  turned everything around sasha loftis  joins us live with her story  denise helen says this entire process  has just been excruciating but the  decision made this week she calls it a  huge step forward for her and anyone  else in a similar situation  it ruined my life helen armstrong shares  the hardest thing she's ever had to go  through i've lost a lot in 2014 she was  working at a local medical practice and  reported to nevada osha what she calls  unsafe practices  including the distribution of expired  drugs and use of contaminated syringes  armstrong says she was told she would  stay anonymous but claims state  officials notified her former bosses of  her claims against them which led to  retaliation and her eventual firing  being fired not having insurance  diagnosed with cancer  wasn't good she tried to get her life  back but hit numerous legal roadblocks  until the u.s court of appeals for the  9th circuit handed her a victory this  week allowing her 2017 lawsuit against  nevada officials to move forward this is  an important precedent attorney john  thai says the unanimous ruling in  helen's favor will also benefit others  in similar situations  every whistleblower  you know has a right to be protected  though she believes this battle is far  from over helen is grateful to finally  feel hurt and she encourages others to  always fight for what they feel is right  know that you have rights out there you  have rights  we did reach out to nevada osha  regarding this case a representative  responded saying they have no comment  now as for that ninth circuit appeal we  have an entire look at that on our  website 8newsnow.com  reporting live in the newsroom tonight  sasha loftis live local now
 

